topic.p:
                    When migrating a handler for one button, subscribe to the corresponding new event and filter arguments.button.
                    For example, replace a subscription to leftButtonDown with:


composition->GetEventReceiver()->mouseDown.AttachLambda(
    [](GuiGraphicsComposition* sender, GuiMouseEventArgs& arguments)
    {
        if (arguments.button != NativeMouseButton::Left) return;
        // Handle the left button press.
    });


                    In GacUI Resource XML, replace ev.leftButtonDown with ev.mouseDown and apply the same filter in Workflow:


<Bounds>
    <ev.mouseDown-eval><![CDATA[
        if (arguments.button == NativeMouseButton::Left)
        {
            arguments.handled = true;
        }
    ]]></ev.mouseDown-eval>
</Bounds>

                    IGuiShortcutKeyManager::TryGetShortcut, CreateNewShortcut, and CreateShortcutIfNotExist
                    add a required bool osSuper parameter between alt and key:


IGuiShortcutKeyItem* TryGetShortcut(bool ctrl, bool shift, bool alt, bool osSuper, VKEY key);
IGuiShortcutKeyItem* CreateNewShortcut(bool ctrl, bool shift, bool alt, bool osSuper, VKEY key);
IGuiShortcutKeyItem* CreateShortcutIfNotExist(bool ctrl, bool shift, bool alt, bool osSuper, VKEY key);


                    Update overrides and callers, including the corresponding methods on GuiShortcutKeyManager.
                    Pass false for existing shortcuts without Win/Command/Super.



class YourElement : public GuiElementBase<TYPE>, YourBaseClasses...
{
    DEFINE_GUI_GRAPHICS_ELEMENT(TYPE, NAME)
...
};


                    should be replaced by


class YourElement : public GuiElementBase<TYPE>, YourBaseClasses...
{
private:
    friend class GuiElementBase<TYPE>;
    static constexpr const wchar_t* ElementTypeName = NAME;
...
};



class YourAllocator : YourBaseClasses...
{
    DEFINE_CACHED_RESOURCE_ALLOCATOR(TKEY, TVALUE)
...
};


                    should be replaced by


class YourAllocator : public GuiCachedResourceAllocatorBase<YourAllocator, TKEY, TVALUE>, YourBaseClasses...
{
...
};



class YourRenderer : public Object, public IGuiGraphicsRenderer, YourBaseClasses...
{
    DEFINE_GUI_GRAPHICS_RENDERER(TELEMENT, TRENDERER, TTARGET)
...
};


                    should be replaced by


class YourRenderer : GuiElementRendererBase<TELEMENT, TRENDERER, TTARGET>, YourBaseClasses...
{
private:
    friend class GuiElementRendererBase<TELEMENT, TRENDERER, TTARGET>;
...
};

                    The nine button-specific callbacks are removed:


LeftButtonDown, MiddleButtonDown, and RightButtonDown are replaced by MouseDown.

LeftButtonUp, MiddleButtonUp, and RightButtonUp are replaced by MouseUp.

LeftButtonDoubleClick, MiddleButtonDoubleClick, and RightButtonDoubleClick are replaced by MouseDoubleClick.

                    The new virtual callbacks take the button as the first parameter:


virtual void MouseDown(NativeMouseButton button, const NativeWindowMouseInfo& info);
virtual void MouseUp(NativeMouseButton button, const NativeWindowMouseInfo& info);
virtual void MouseDoubleClick(NativeMouseButton button, const NativeWindowMouseInfo& info);

WindowMouseInfo and NativeWindowMouseInfo are aliases of WindowMouseInfo_<T>, so both gain this field.
GuiMouseEventArgs, GuiKeyEventArgs, and GuiCharEventArgs inherit osSuper from their input-info base types;
GuiItemMouseEventArgs and GuiNodeMouseEventArgs inherit it through GuiMouseEventArgs.

RegisterGlobalShortcutKey adds a required bool osSuper parameter between alt and key:
virtual vint RegisterGlobalShortcutKey(bool ctrl, bool shift, bool alt, bool osSuper, VKEY key) = 0;
Update implementations and callers. Pass false for global shortcuts without Win/Command/Super. A Windows provider maps osSuper to MOD_WIN when registering the hot key.
